 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Deblur-GS Implementation
-Deblur-GS is a specific implementation of differentiable pose estimation designed for camera motion blurred images [S1]. It extends the differentiable Gaussian splatting rasterizer to support depth, pose, and cumulative opacity for both forward and backward propagation [S1]. It uses two separate Adam optimizers for Gaussian scene parameters and camera pose parameters [S1]. The camera trajectory is modeled using 7th-order Bézier interpolation with 31 sampled points, and control points are initialized via COLMAP and optimized during training [S1]. It was trained for 90K iterations on a single NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU [S1].
+A specific implementation of differentiable pose estimation designed for motion-blurred images [S1]. It extends the differentiable Gaussian splatting rasterizer to support depth, pose, and cumulative opacity for both forward and backward propagation [S1]. It uses two separate Adam optimizers for Gaussian scene parameters and camera pose parameters [S1]. The pose learning rate exponentially decays from $1 \times 10^{-3}$ to $1 \times 10^{-5}$ [S1]. Camera motion is modeled using 7th-order Bézier interpolation with 31 sampled points on the trajectory [S1]. Training runs for 90K iterations on a single NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU [S1].
+
+### Evaluation-time Pose Alignment
+A post-processing step used in pose-free reconstruction settings where ground-truth poses are unavailable [S2, unofficial]. Since multiple 3D scenes and camera configurations can explain the same unposed inputs, the reconstructed scene may not align with the ground-truth evaluation frame [S2, unofficial]. To resolve this, the reconstructed 3D Gaussians are frozen, and each target camera pose is optimized for 200 iterations using an L2 loss [S2, unofficial]. This alignment is not used if ground-truth poses are available [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians $N$ and the number of pixels. The rasterization is $O(N)$ per pixel, and the gradient computation is linear in the number of visible Gaussians [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Stores gradients for all Gaussians and camera parameters. The extension to support pose gradients in the rasterizer increases memory overhead slightly compared to standard 3DGS [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Evaluated on synthetic (ExBluRF) and real datasets [S1]. It achieves competitive deblurring and novel view synthesis performance compared to Deblur-NeRF and BAD-NeRF [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Robustness to elongated Gaussians by ignoring the covariance term; stability through simplified gradient derivation [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the assumption that color remains unchanged during short pose steps; may not converge if initial poses are poor or if the motion blur model is inaccurate [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When dealing with motion-blurred images and needing to recover camera poses jointly with the scene representation [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians $N$ and the rasterization cost. Each iteration requires a forward render and backward pass. Deblur-GS uses 90K iterations [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Stores Gaussian parameters and gradients for both scene and pose. The extension to support depth and cumulative opacity increases memory overhead slightly [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Robust to elongated Gaussian shapes by ignoring covariance gradients [S1]. Efficiently handles pose optimization by leveraging position-pose equivalence [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Assumes short optimization steps for color invariance [S1]. Requires good initialization (e.g., COLMAP) [S1]. Pose-free settings require additional evaluation-time alignment [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When optimizing camera poses for a static scene with known or roughly estimated initial poses, particularly in deblurring or novel view synthesis contexts [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** For online SLAM where incremental tracking is required; use [SplaTAM](9193-splatam.md) or [Gaussian-SLAM](9195-gaussian-slam.md) instead.
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the paper "Deblur-GS: 3D Gaussian Splatting from Camera Motion Blurred Images" by Chen and Liu et al. [S1]. It builds upon the differentiable rendering capabilities of 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1]. The approach addresses the limitations of NeRF-based methods (like Deblur-NeRF) by leveraging the explicit representation of 3D Gaussians for more efficient and stable optimization [S1].
+The method originates from the need to handle camera motion blur in 3D Gaussian Splatting, leading to the Deblur-GS paper [S1]. It builds on the differentiable rasterization of 3DGS [S1]. The equivalence between camera transformation and Gaussian position transformation is a key theoretical insight enabling efficient gradient computation [S1]. Evaluation-time pose alignment protocols were later formalized in pose-free reconstruction benchmarks [S2, unofficial].
 
